## [C] Answer the following

### (a) Write a program which calls a function `cls()`. This function should be capable of clearing any part of the screen. If the function is called without any arguments then it should clear the screen.

**Answer:**
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// Global constants for terminal dimensions
const int SCREEN_WIDTH = 80;
const int SCREEN_HEIGHT = 40;

/**
 * Clears the entire screen by printing multiple newlines.
 * Overloaded version with no arguments.
 */
void cls() {
 // Print enough newlines to push existing text out of view
 for (int i = 0; i < SCREEN_HEIGHT; ++i) {
 std::cout << "\n";
 }
 std::cout << std::flush;
}

/**
 * Clears a specific rectangular region of the screen using spaces.
 * @param x The starting column (0-indexed from left)
 * @param y The starting row (0-indexed from top)
 * @param width The number of columns to clear
 * @param height The number of rows to clear
 */
void cls(int x, int y, int width, int height) {
 std::string spaces(width, ' ');
 // Loop through each row of the target area
 for (int row = 0; row < height; ++row) {
 // Move the cursor to the target row and column using ANSI escape codes
 std::cout << (y + row + 1) << ";" << (x + 1) << "H";
 // Overwrite the text in that row with spaces
 std::cout << spaces;
 }
 std::cout << std::flush;
}

int main() {
 // 1. Fill the screen with test pattern text
 cls();
 for (int i = 0; i < 20; ++i) {
 std::cout << "Line " << (i < 10 ? "0" : "") << i 
 << ": OOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOO\n";
 }
 std::cout << "\nPress Enter to clear a specific box (X:10, Y:3, W:20, H:5) using spaces...";
 std::cin.get();
 
 // 2. Clear a specific section (Column 10, Row 3, Width 20, Height 5)
 cls(10, 3, 20, 5);
 std::cout << "Press Enter to clear the entire screen using newlines...";
 std::cin.get();
 
 // 3. Clear the whole screen using the newline method
 cls();
 std::cout << "Screen cleared using newlines!\n";
 return 0;
}
```

